course: dictionary
lesson: 3475
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have many meanings, so you must use the context to find the right one. The word fall is a good example. «Mercury's daytime surface reaches about 430 degrees Celsius, but at night it falls to about minus 180.» Here fall means to go down in amount, like a temperature. The numbers are the clue.
explain: «Astronauts float because they and their station are falling round the Earth together.» Here fall means to drop toward the ground, pulled down by gravity. The station is not landing, though. The clue is round the Earth: they keep falling but keep moving around it.
explain: «Grown-ups fall for these every day, which is exactly why the tricks are used.» Fall for means to be tricked by something. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: grown-ups are tricked by these every day, which makes sense. If it did not fit, you would try another meaning.
fact: To fall means to drop down toward the ground, and a fall is a time when someone or something drops.
fact: To fall can also mean to go down in amount, like prices or temperatures. The past tense is fell, as in «The price fell by 5 pounds.»
fact: In the USA and Canada, fall is another word for autumn, the season after summer, when leaves fall.
fact: A waterfall is a fall of water, like Niagara Falls. To fall asleep means to begin to sleep.
fact: Fall apart means to break into pieces or stop working, as in «The rumour falls apart by the second question.»
exercise: «Mercury's night temperature falls to about minus 180.» In this sentence, fall means: | Begin to sleep | Drop onto the ground | *Go down in amount, like a temperature
exercise: «Grown-ups fall for these every day.» What does fall for mean here? | Drop down toward the ground | Go down in amount | *Are tricked by
exercise: In the USA and Canada, what does fall mean? | A drop in prices | To fall asleep | *Autumn, the season after summer | A waterfall
test: A price fell by 5 pounds. What happened to the price? | Stayed the same | *Went down | Went up
test: Ella began to fall asleep on the sofa. What does fall asleep mean? | Fell off the sofa | *Began to sleep | Woke up suddenly
exam: «The rumour fell apart by the second question.» What does fall apart mean here? | Became much louder | *Broke down and stopped being believable | Grew into a waterfall | Went up in amount
